PART 1 — Network Sniffing (Diagnose Connectivity Issues)
▶  Set Up Your Lab Network
    Make sure Kali Linux and Metasploitable 2 are running on the same Host-Only network.
    Both machines should be able to ping each other before you start.
    Note your Kali IP (ifconfig) and Metasploitable IP — you will need these.
▶  Capture Traffic with Wireshark (GUI)
    Open Wireshark from Kali Applications → Sniffing & Spoofing → Wireshark.
    Select your network interface (usually eth0 or the Host-Only adapter).
    Click the blue shark-fin Start button to begin capturing.
    Generate some traffic: open a browser, ping Metasploitable, browse its web server.
    Stop the capture after 1-2 minutes.
▶  Analyse the Capture in Wireshark
    Use the filter bar at the top to isolate specific traffic:
http                    # Show only HTTP traffic
tcp                     # Show only TCP packets
icmp                    # Show only ping packets
ip.addr == 192.168.x.x  # Filter by specific IP address
    Look for: retransmissions (sign of packet loss), connection resets (RST flags), slow responses, or unusual protocols.
    Right-click any TCP stream → Follow → TCP Stream to read full conversation.
▶  Capture Traffic with tcpdump (Command Line)
    Open a terminal in Kali and run:
sudo tcpdump -i eth0                          # Capture all traffic
sudo tcpdump -i eth0 -w capture.pcap          # Save to file
sudo tcpdump -i eth0 host 192.168.56.101      # Filter by host
sudo tcpdump -i eth0 port 80                  # Filter by port
wireshark capture.pcap                        # Open saved capture in Wireshark
    Now capture traffic in Wireshark while the MitM is active. You will see both sides of the conversation.
    When done, stop arpspoof and reset ip_forward to 0.


▶  Diagnose & Document Findings
    Based on your packet capture, write a diagnosis report:
Protocols Observed on the Network
During packet capture using Wireshark/tcpdump, the following protocols were observed:
•	ICMP (Internet Control Message Protocol):
Used for network connectivity testing (ping requests and replies between Kali and Metasploitable). 
•	TCP (Transmission Control Protocol):
Used for establishing reliable communication sessions between client and server. 
•	HTTP (Hypertext Transfer Protocol):
Used for web traffic communication on port 80

        - What protocols were seen on the network?
Presence of Plaintext Credentials / Sensitive Data
Upon inspecting HTTP traffic using “Follow TCP Stream”:
•	User data (such as login credentials) was transmitted in plaintext format. 
•	Example observed pattern:
username=admin
password=admin

        - Were any credentials or sensitive data visible in plaintext?
Based on packet analysis, the potential causes include:
•	Packet loss indicated by TCP retransmissions 
•	TCP connection resets (RST flags), suggesting unstable sessions or service interruptions 
•	Possible server-side service instability or overload on Metasploitable 
•	Insecure protocol usage (HTTP instead of HTTPS), exposing communication vulnerabilities

        - What is the likely root cause of the connectivity problem?
Network instability caused by packet loss and TCP connection resets, likely due to misconfigured or overloaded services in the virtual lab environment (Host-Only network + Metasploitable service behavior).

        - What solutions do you recommend? (e.g. use HTTPS, switch to SSH, fix DNS config)

Security Improvements
•	Replace HTTP with HTTPS to encrypt data transmission. 
•	Use SSH instead of Telnet/unencrypted services. 
•	Avoid sending sensitive credentials in plaintext


